<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kazakh SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1936 CE – 1991 CE · capital Alma-Ata</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The vast Soviet republic of the Kazakhs, scene of the Virgin Lands campaign, nuclear testing at Semipalatinsk and the Baikonur space launches.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazakh-assr">Kazakh ASSR</a> became a union republic, the Kazakh SSR, under the Soviet constitution of December 1936, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Alma-Ata</a>. It emerged from the famine of the early 1930s with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> a minority in their own land.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic received deported peoples, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-germans">Volga Germans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> camps such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlag">Karlag</a>. From 1954 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgin-lands-campaign">Virgin Lands campaign</a> ploughed millions of hectares of steppe for wheat, bringing in Russian and Ukrainian settlers. The Soviet nuclear test site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semey">Semipalatinsk</a> carried out over 450 tests from 1949 to 1989, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baikonur-cosmodrome">Baikonur</a> launched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinmukhamed-kunaev">Dinmukhamed Kunaev</a> led the republic from 1964 to 1986.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinmukhamed-kunaev">Kunaev</a>'s replacement by a Russian sparked the Jeltoqsan protests of December 1986. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nursultan-nazarbayev">Nursultan Nazarbayev</a> became leader in 1989, and on 16 December 1991 the republic, the last to leave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, declared independence as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kazakh-ssr.jpg" alt="Kazakh SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kazakh_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1953%E2%80%931991);_Flag_of_Kazakhstan_(1991%E2%80%931992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1936 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alma-Ata</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dinmukhamed Kunaev, Nursultan Nazarbayev</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (state atheism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kazakh, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kazakh Soviet Socialist Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1964&amp;focus=kazakh-ssr" aria-label="Kazakh SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6263 497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kazakh SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6583 753L6853 898M6583 753L5820 533M6583 753L6622 661"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6853 898h0M5820 533h0M6622 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6583 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6583 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1964&amp;focus=kazakh-ssr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
